<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch04_P01_Right_angle_at_B</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>In the text, \(P\) is \(AB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\), and \(Q\) says that the angle at \(B\) is a right angle; then \(P \Leftrightarrow Q\).</p>
<p>Suppose instead that \(Q\) said only ‘triangle \(ABC\) is right-angled’. Then \(Q \Rightarrow P\) would be false. Show this with a counterexample: give the side lengths of an example of a right-angled triangle \(ABC\) for which \(AB^2 + BC^2 \neq AC^2\).</p>
<div>\(AB =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>\(BC =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
<div>\(AC =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</div>
<p>Type \(\sqrt{2}\) as <code>sqrt(2)</code>.</p>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A counterexample to \(Q \Rightarrow P\) needs \(Q\) true and \(P\) false: a triangle that is right-angled, but for which \(AB^2 + BC^2 \neq AC^2\).</p>
<p>Put the right angle at \(A\): \(AB = 3\), \(AC = 4\), \(BC = 5\). Then \(AB^2 + AC^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = BC^2\), so the triangle is right-angled, but \(AB^2 + BC^2 = 9 + 25 = 34\) and \(AC^2 = 16\). So ‘\(ABC\) is right-angled’ does not imply \(AB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\).</p>
<p>By Pythagoras' theorem, \(AB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\) only when the right angle is at \(B\), opposite \(AC\). So \(Q\) has to say where the right angle is. With \(Q\) ‘the angle at \(B\) is a right angle’, \(Q \Rightarrow P\) is Pythagoras' theorem and \(P \Rightarrow Q\) is its converse.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
