    <node><name>2</name><description>Diagnostic: the right angle at B makes P true</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>atB</sans><tans>false</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>1.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-3-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Yes. The triangle is right-angled, so \(Q\) is true, but \(AB^2 + BC^2 = {@ev(sa^2 + sb^2, simp)@}\) and \(AC^2 = {@ev(sc^2, simp)@}\), so \(P\) is false. The right angle is at [[if test="atA"]]\(A\)[[/if]][[if test="atC"]]\(C\)[[/if]], not \(B\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-3-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your triangle has its right angle at \(B\), so \(AB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\): \(P\) is true as well as \(Q\). Put the right angle at \(A\) or at \(C\) instead.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
